// The test project: checked by `npm run typecheck`, never by `npm run build`.
//
// Two things here look wrong and are not:
//
// 1. It re-`include`s `src` instead of declaring `references: [tsconfig.app.json]`, which
//    is the usual way to express "the tests depend on the app". A reference is rejected
//    outright — `error TS6310: Referenced project may not disable emit` — because the app
//    project sets `noEmit: true`, and a referenced project has to emit the .d.ts files its
//    dependents consume. The alternative is `emitDeclarationOnly` on the app project, which
//    would make every production build write declarations nothing reads. Since a composite
//    project must list every file in its program, and the tests import `src` directly,
//    listing `src` here is the cheaper trade.
//
// 2. Because of (1), a type error in `src` is reported twice by `npm run typecheck` — once
//    per project. Cosmetic, and only when something is already broken; `npm run build`
//    reports it once.
{
  "extends": "./tsconfig.app.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    // Must differ from the app project's: two projects sharing one .tsbuildinfo clobber
    // each other's incremental state and silently skip checks.
    "tsBuildInfoFile": "./node_modules/.tmp/tsconfig.test.tsbuildinfo"
  },
  "include": [
    "test",
    "vitest.config.ts",
    "src",
    "scripts",
    "package.json"
  ]
}
